1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,704
A storm takes out a feeder, and a
neighborhood goes dark.

2
00:00:03,704 --> 00:00:05,776
Here's the whole board before anyone

3
00:00:05,776 --> 00:00:10,455
touches it — a hospital burning through
its backup, a shelter filling with people,

4
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:13,530
and a water pump that dies the moment the
battery does.

5
00:00:13,530 --> 00:00:15,642
The operator has minutes to choose a

6
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:19,583
switching sequence — and the wrong switch
energizes a live fault.

7
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:23,692
Ask a chatbot, and you get confident prose
about a grid it has never seen.

8
00:00:23,692 --> 00:00:25,637
Point a screen-clicking agent at this

9
00:00:25,637 --> 00:00:28,796
diagram, and it's guessing which circle
isolates the fault.

10
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:30,845
So this page stops being a picture.

11
00:00:30,845 --> 00:00:33,583
It publishes its own domain as nine WebMCP

12
00:00:33,583 --> 00:00:37,338
tools, running on the same engine as the
human controls.

13
00:00:37,338 --> 00:00:38,461
One request.

14
00:00:38,461 --> 00:00:43,294
Restore the critical loads, keep the fault
isolated, hold twenty-five percent battery

15
00:00:43,294 --> 00:00:46,968
reserve, and show me what happens if it
tries to execute without me.

16
00:00:46,968 --> 00:00:50,833
It reads the incident and the interlocks
by name, not by pixel.

17
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:53,727
Then it puts the tempting plan next to the
safe one.

18
00:00:53,727 --> 00:00:56,200
The bigger plan restores more kilowatts —

19
00:00:56,200 --> 00:01:00,509
and the page blocks it, because reserve
collapses to eighteen percent.

20
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:02,072
That's not the model's opinion.

21
00:01:02,072 --> 00:01:03,634
Those are the page's rules.

22
00:01:03,634 --> 00:01:09,155
The safer plan lands in preview: amber,
reversible, nothing actually switched.

23
00:01:09,155 --> 00:01:12,708
Then it attempts execution, and the page
stops it cold.

24
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:16,204
Here's where this becomes collaboration
instead of automation.

25
00:01:16,204 --> 00:01:17,639
I don't rewrite the prompt.

26
00:01:17,639 --> 00:01:18,854
I use the website.

27
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:20,440
Water pump: critical.

28
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:22,966
That one click advances the shared state,

29
00:01:22,966 --> 00:01:25,926
marks the plan stale, and revokes any
approval.

30
00:01:25,926 --> 00:01:30,729
The agent gets a structured error instead
of a silent wrong answer, and re-plans

31
00:01:30,729 --> 00:01:35,532
around my judgment — all three critical
services, twenty-six percent reserve.

32
00:01:35,532 --> 00:01:38,241
It can stage the exact switching sequence.

33
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:39,444
It can never approve it.

34
00:01:39,444 --> 00:01:44,473
Authorization is a button on the page that
mints a short-lived grant bound to this

35
00:01:44,473 --> 00:01:48,044
exact plan, and that secret never touches
model context.

36
00:01:48,044 --> 00:01:48,924
Approved.

37
00:01:48,924 --> 00:01:50,220
One execution.

38
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,584
Hospital, shelter, and water come up, the

39
00:01:52,584 --> 00:01:56,976
fault stays isolated, and the receipt
records who approved what — down to the

40
00:01:56,976 --> 00:01:59,745
plan hash and the state versions on either
side.

41
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:03,588
Retry with the same key, and it refuses to
run twice.

42
00:02:03,588 --> 00:02:06,921
This is a training simulator, not utility
software.

43
00:02:06,921 --> 00:02:08,472
But the pattern is the point.

44
00:02:08,472 --> 00:02:11,088
Agents don't earn trust by clicking more
carefully.

45
00:02:11,088 --> 00:02:13,230
They earn it when the page gives them a

46
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:15,787
language to speak — and keeps the last
word.
